## Idea 1 Girvi Formalizer
- NO competitor doing pawnbroker-conversion found. BUT closest analogs: acquisitions of gold NBFC books: L&T Finance bought Paul Merchants Finance gold loan biz for INR 537 cr (book INR 1,350 cr, 130 branches, ~700 staff) Jun 2025; InCred bought TruCap gold biz (115 branches, INR 650 cr AUM) Feb 2025; Tata Capital ~88.6% of Yogakshemam (Jul 2026); Godrej Capital took Kanakadurga Finance. Buyers = big NBFCs, so deal competition for ANY sizeable book exists. https://www.theweek.in/wire-updates/business/2025/06/09/dcm14-biz-briefs.html ; https://www.theweek.in/wire-updates/business/2025/02/25/dcm88-biz-incred-gold.html
- Franchise format already exists: IIFL Capital franchisee branches in TN (IIFL Gold loan franchise); Indel/others use agents. Muthoot, Manappuram, Kosamattam are themselves ex-pawnbrokers. 
- Legal: unincorporated pawnbrokers sit under state Pawnbrokers/Moneylenders Acts, outside RBI perimeter => asset/business purchase avoids change-of-control (inference). Acquiring a dormant small NBFC DOES need RBI prior approval (~7 months in Capital India example). New-entrant NBFC licence needs NOF 10 cr etc.
- Weakness: Seller's goodwill = the 24-48% spread; if realised loss under 0.5% claim wrong, books fail. Unquantified count of 'willing sellers'. $95M equity+$550M debt for $130M revenue; lender economics: net margin after 10-11% funding cost + opex ~ low; revenue is interest income.
- Rupeek (informal->formal thesis): >$164-209M raised, 9 yrs, AUM INR 1,864 cr Sep 2024. Pool says profit INR15cr H1.

## Idea 2 Bullion-Bid
- Competitor: iGOLD (Bangalore, founded Aug 2017, online auctions of pledged gold for banks/NBFCs, aggregates KYC'd jewellers, prototype stage 7 staff per Gust listing) https://gust.com/companies/igold . Banks already route e-auctions via third-party 'approved auction service provider' (IndusInd notice; WBGB notice Oct 2024 names an 'egold' portal). Muthoot/Shriram run own digital auctions (Shriram: 'how the digital gold loan auction process works' 2026). AGLC (Association of Gold Loan Companies) standard auction notice.
- Premise check: auction volume depends on NPAs. Muthoot stage III 2.28% and falling => small flow. Normal-year auction volume = lender recovery of ~2% of stock ($4B) x take 2.5%?? => ~$100M gross only at 40% share in stress year; pool admits normal-year is a quarter. Auctions are 'as is' sales to local jewellers who need KYC; lenders get 90% reserve anyway.

## Idea 1 extra (legal premise)
- Tamil Nadu Pawn Brokers Act 1943 (as amended 1979/1992): pawnbroker interest capped at state-notified rate tied to RBI bank rate, ceiling = bank rate + 5 pts. Could NOT find current notified rate. If licensed TN pawnbrokers charge near cap (~low-teens %), NBFC 18-22% is MORE expensive => premise 'half the informal rate' fails for licensed shops; it only holds for unlicensed/off-book lenders. https://www.casemine.com/act/in/5a9cce174a93265347813203 ; https://www.casemine.com/act/in/5a9cce174a93265347813205
- Formal NBFC yields: Muthoot ~16.3%, Manappuram ~16.6% (loan rate), IIFL/Capri yields ~18.6% Q1FY27; SBI ~9.6%. Four largest gold NBFC share fell 90%->70% in 4 yrs. https://trendlyne.com/posts/5826992/indias-gold-loan-rush-bigger-books-tighter-margins
- No 2026 evidence pawnbrokers are in distress; IIFL ops head: stricter rules push customers to unorganised.
- Verdict weak. median $15M upside $60M (200 shops x INR5cr x ~19% = $22M median-ish; 500 shops x 6cr = INR3000cr=$320M x20% = $65M upside).

